        <mj-raw>=0D=0A                                    <div class=3D"new=
s-content">=0D=0A                                      <p><a href=3D"http:/=
/post.spmailtechnolo.com/f/a/SqzgWJ6TlW2nxn0CdSmRuw~~/AABEfgA~/RgRm4evSP0QW=
aHR0cHM6Ly92aWV3cG9ydHMuZnlpL1cDc3BjQgpk-ttm_2QGZZgAUhNsYXZhLm1vYmlAZ21haWw=
uY29tWAQAAAAA">I think the nail is in coffin now</a>: you should never desi=
gn something for the web with only one (or even a narrow set) of particular=
 viewport sizes in mind. It=E2=80=99s just so <em>darn tempting</em> to thi=
nk that way. You have a couple of pretty specific screen sizes in front of =
you right now, you likely design toward those to some degree. Design tools =
often ask you to draw a rectangle that represent a screen to design for. Te=
sting tools sometimes show you a site at a set of pre-set screen sizes. It =
can feel normal and fine to design toward, say, three sizes, and hone in on=
 them. Honestly, that might end up working fine, but it might not! It might=
 lead to some awkward in-betweens, especially if you are very rigid in writ=
ing CSS that only changes at those specific breakpoints only.</p>=0A=0A<p>T=
hat=E2=80=99s the thing, really. You just don=E2=80=99t have to think in re=
ally specific breakpoints anymore. Media query width breakpoints are still =
a fine tool, but now we=E2=80=99ve got viewport units, container units, con=
tainer queries, calc/min/max/clamp, and all sorts of other stuff that allow=
 you to design components and pages that work well and look good at the siz=
e and under the conditions they are in. It=E2=80=99s just a better way to c=
ode. But this stuff has only relatively recently arrived in CSS so it=E2=80=
=99ll take a minute for it all to settle in.</p>=0A=0A<p>This isn=E2=80=99t=
 even really <em>new</em> news. <a href=3D"http://post.spmailtechnolo.com/f=
/a/t1nKUUt6ncAVLtxrc_ys_g~~/AABEfgA~/RgRm4evSP0RDaHR0cHM6Ly9jc3MtdHJpY2tzLm=
NvbS9zY3JlZW4tcmVzb2x1dGlvbi1ub3RlcXVhbHRvLWJyb3dzZXItd2luZG93L1cDc3BjQgpk-=
ttm_2QGZZgAUhNsYXZhLm1vYmlAZ21haWwuY29tWAQAAAAA">Over a decade ago</a>, I w=
as like, yo, there are a ton of different sizes that your site is getting v=
iewed at. Deal with it. Now we can properly.</p>=0A=0A<hr />=0A<marquee sty=
le=3D"background: black; color: white; border-block: 1px solid black; margi=
n: 40px 0; padding: 20px; text-transform: uppercase;">And now for something=
 completely different</marquee>=0A<hr />=0A=0A<p>Have websites gone to crap=
=3F Browse around <em>popular</em> sites, and I think you=E2=80=99ll land o=
n an easy yes. Especially on mobile, cripes. Just to name a few: they are t=
oo slow to load, the ads and popups are too obtrusive, and there is too muc=
h usage of fixed-position elements that reduce usable area.</p>=0A<p>This w=
ebsite <a href=3D"http://post.spmailtechnolo.com/f/a/JeZ71mcoW5Y1Ql7brpZoeA=
~~/AABEfgA~/RgRm4evSP0QjaHR0cHM6Ly91c2VyaW55ZXJmYWNlLmNvbS9nYW1lLmh0bWxXA3N=
wY0IKZPrbZv9kBmWYAFITbGF2YS5tb2JpQGdtYWlsLmNvbVgEAAAAAA~~">User Inyerface</=
a> satirized it recently, and it=E2=80=99s pretty funny (ya know, if being =
intentionally frustrated is your thing, gamers should relate).</p>=0A=0A<p>=
People have been worried about this for ages, and it never seems to get any=
 better.</p>=0A=0A<ul>=0A<li>Brad Frost called it <a href=3D"http://post.sp=
mailtechnolo.com/f/a/rgyeeo4adgjMt3wPOwBrnA~~/AABEfgA~/RgRm4evSP0QcaHR0cHM6=
Ly9kZWF0aHRvYnVsbHNoaXQuY29tL1cDc3BjQgpk-ttm_2QGZZgAUhNsYXZhLm1vYmlAZ21haWw=
uY29tWAQAAAAA">BS</a>.</li>=0A<li>A couple folks made <a href=3D"http://pos=
t.spmailtechnolo.com/f/a/O2ydBLr-_nMSWf0cLwqBwA~~/AABEfgA~/RgRm4evSP0QkaHR0=
cHM6Ly93d3cudGVybXNhbmRjb25kaXRpb25zLmdhbWUvVwNzcGNCCmT622b_ZAZlmABSE2xhdmE=
ubW9iaUBnbWFpbC5jb21YBAAAAAA~">termsandconditions.game</a> mining <a href=
=3D"http://post.spmailtechnolo.com/f/a/7gBpT48Jn4Jfk-uNCEj3xQ~~/AABEfgA~/Rg=
Rm4evSP0QqaHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZGVjZXB0aXZlLmRlc2lnbi9oYWxsLW9mLXNoYW1lVwNzcGNCC=
mT622b_ZAZlmABSE2xhdmEubW9iaUBnbWFpbC5jb21YBAAAAAA~">these patterns</a> for=
 ideas.</li>=0A<li>Guangyi Li=E2=80=99s <a href=3D"http://post.spmailtechno=
lo.com/f/a/Y1NA_ke7UbFV7cVaUBS5Eg~~/AABEfgA~/RgRm4evSP0QnaHR0cHM6Ly9ob3ctaS=
1leHBlcmllbmNlLXdlYi10b2RheS5jb20vVwNzcGNCCmT622b_ZAZlmABSE2xhdmEubW9iaUBnb=
WFpbC5jb21YBAAAAAA~">how-i-experience-web-today.com</a> gets at the heart o=
f the complete experience.</li>=0A<li>Tracking is a big part of this story,=
 and <a href=3D"http://post.spmailtechnolo.com/f/a/RZqS8nADHuobEWanRo7O0A~~=
/AABEfgA~/RgRm4evSP0QeaHR0cHM6Ly9jbGlja2NsaWNrY2xpY2suY2xpY2svVwNzcGNCCmT62=
2b_ZAZlmABSE2xhdmEubW9iaUBnbWFpbC5jb21YBAAAAAA~">clickclickclick.click</a> =
pokes at that.</li>=0A</ul>=0A=0A<p>This all just makes me sad. Fortunately=
, most things are <a href=3D"http://post.spmailtechnolo.com/f/a/V2a-HPEeqJm=
HdKl9nO1_Dg~~/AABEfgA~/RgRm4evSP0Q_aHR0cHM6Ly9jaHJpc2NveWllci5uZXQvMjAyMy8w=
OC8zMS9hLWxvdC1vZi1zdHVmZi1pcy1qdXN0LWZpbmUvVwNzcGNCCmT622b_ZAZlmABSE2xhdmE=
ubW9iaUBnbWFpbC5jb21YBAAAAAA~">fine</a>.</p>=0A=0A<hr />=0A<marquee style=
=3D"background: black; color: white; border-block: 1px solid black; margin:=
 40px 0; padding: 20px; text-transform: uppercase;">And now for something c=
ompletely different</marquee>=0A<hr />=0A=0A<p>Have you seen <a href=3D"htt=
p://post.spmailtechnolo.com/f/a/9cgzYRLTNMxvQrnYAeJvWg~~/AABEfgA~/RgRm4evSP=
0Q6aHR0cHM6Ly9kZXZlbG9wZXIuY2hyb21lLmNvbS9ibG9nL2ludHJvZHVjaW5nLXBvcG92ZXIt=
YXBpL1cDc3BjQgpk-ttm_2QGZZgAUhNsYXZhLm1vYmlAZ21haWwuY29tWAQAAAAA">the popov=
er API</a>=3F It=E2=80=99s a neat idea, already play-with-able in Chrome. T=
hink styled tooltips. The idea is that you connect some interaction (click =
of a button) to toggling another element with more information or context. =
Amazingly, to me, this HTML totally works in Chrome with no CSS or JavaScri=
pt at all:</p>=0A<pre><code>=0A&lt;button popovertarget=3D"my-popover">Open=
 Popover&lt;/button>=0A=0A&lt;div id=3D"my-popover" popover>=0A  &lt;p>I am=
 a popover with more information.&lt;/p>=0A&lt;/div></code></pre>=0A=0A<p>Y=
ou can style stuff with CSS of course, but the basics of the interaction wo=
rk without. Like a <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> element.</p>=0A=0A<p>Anytim=
e we get any form of =E2=80=9Cstate management=E2=80=9D outside of JavaScri=
pt, the people will play! There are countless games made in CSS thanks to t=
he whole idea of the <code>:checked</code> selector in CSS and using the <c=
ode>~</code> combinator to select other elements.</p>=0A=0A<p>This time, le=
ave it to <a href=3D"http://post.spmailtechnolo.com/f/a/UEPdnH208Y6tHDcJqGb=
Y-w~~/AABEfgA~/RgRm4evSP0QwaHR0cHM6Ly9wb3J0c3dpZ2dlci5uZXQvYmxvZy90aWMtdGFj=
LXRvZS1pbi1odG1sVwNzcGNCCmT622b_ZAZlmABSE2xhdmEubW9iaUBnbWFpbC5jb21YBAAAAAA=
~">Garth Heyes who has made Tic-Tac-Toe</a> entirely in HTML only. That=E2=
=80=99s gotta be a first.</p>=0A=0A<p>Wanna see it=3F Fair warning first. I=
t=E2=80=99s 170 MB (!!) of HTML and =E2=80=9Cover half a million nodes=E2=
=80=9D. Chrome really struggles with this. It took my machine maybe near a =
minute to even render the first page, and each click took a while as well. =
If you=E2=80=99re down try it, <a href=3D"http://post.spmailtechnolo.com/f/=
a/f08Qr6s0gqUzyYT1aP6k5w~~/AABEfgA~/RgRm4evSP0QsaHR0cHM6Ly9nYXJldGhoZXllcy5=
