ackground: black; color: white; border-block: 1px solid black; margin: 40px=
 0; padding: 20px; text-transform: uppercase;">And now for something relate=
d but different</marquee><hr />=0A=0A<p>So now that we=E2=80=99ve looked at=
 something you absolutely shouldn=E2=80=99t do on the web, here=E2=80=99s <=
a href=3D"http://post.spmailtechnolo.com/f/a/ofqw9DftiTD_7nyZ6pbneg~~/AABEf=
gA~/RgRm4evSP0Q_aHR0cHM6Ly9oZWF0aGVyLWJ1Y2hlbC5jb20vYmxvZy8yMDIzLzA3L2p1c3Q=
tbm9ybWFsLXdlYi10aGluZ3MvVwNzcGNCCmT622b_ZAZlmABSE2xhdmEubW9iaUBnbWFpbC5jb2=
1YBAAAAAA~">Heather Buchel with some things you absolutely <em>should</em> =
do on the web</a>. Heather ain=E2=80=99t even mad that we=E2=80=99re buildi=
ng websites with newfangled tech and trying to share code across platforms =
and all that, but, just, like, don=E2=80=99t break stuff. Don=E2=80=99t bre=
ak <em>super duper basic stuff</em> that websites easily do and are good fo=
r everyone. I=E2=80=99ll hijack her whole list, but of course go read it fo=
r more context:</p>=0A=0A<ul>=0A<li>Let me copy text so I can paste it.</li=
>=0A<li>If something navigates like a link, let me do link things.</li>=0A<=
li>Let me zoom in on my browser without the website getting all out of whac=
k.</li>=0A<li>Do responsive things.</li>=0A<li>Let me have hover styles.</l=
i>=0A<li>If the UI completely changes when I click on something, as if I=E2=
=80=99ve navigated to a new page, give me a browser history update and a ne=
w URL.</li>=0A<li>Let me see scroll bars.</li>=0A<li>Stop hijacking my typi=
cal browser shortcuts for use in your own app.</li>=0A</ul>=0A=0A<p>Reasona=
ble asks, no=3F</p>=0A=0A<hr />=0A<marquee style=3D"background: black; colo=
r: white; border-block: 1px solid black; margin: 40px 0; padding: 20px; tex=
t-transform: uppercase;">And now for something along those same lines</marq=
uee><hr />=0A=0A<p>Onnnnneeee more thing you should be really careful about=
 doing on the web. Adam Silver: <a href=3D"http://post.spmailtechnolo.com/f=
/a/WED0WBiNTR2_tFm4WfhP3g~~/AABEfgA~/RgRm4evSP0RQaHR0cHM6Ly9hZGFtc2lsdmVyLm=
lvL2Jsb2cvdGhlLXByb2JsZW0td2l0aC1zdGlja3ktbWVudXMtYW5kLXdoYXQtdG8tZG8taW5zd=
GVhZC9XA3NwY0IKZPrbZv9kBmWYAFITbGF2YS5tb2JpQGdtYWlsLmNvbVgEAAAAAA~~">The pr=
oblem with sticky menus and what to do instead</a>.</p>=0A=0A<p>One problem=
 is fairly obvious with sticky menus: they overlap stuff! They get in the d=
ang way far too often.</p>=0A<p>But there are other things that cause probl=
ems that you might not see right away. Adam mentions zooming. One little zo=
om or too might kick a sticky/fixed element right off the page. Also, if so=
mething opens a sticky menu, and that menu happens to be taller than the vi=
ewport, you=E2=80=99ve got issues. You either need <em>that</em> area to be=
 scrollable (but nested scrolling sucks) or you require users to scroll lik=
ely further than they want to just to see more of the menu. Ughghadk.</p>=
=0A=0A<p>Adam lists three more that are just as bad or worse, and even less=
 obvious at first glance. I=E2=80=99ll force you over there to see them. Bu=
t I=E2=80=99ll snag the good ending, featuring the alternatives:</p>=0A=0A<=
blockquote><ol>=0A<li><strong>Keep pages short</strong>: Sticky menus are a=
 symptom of long pages so fix the root cause.</li>=0A<li><strong>Just let u=
sers scroll</strong>: It=E2=80=99s a myth that scrolling is a problem. Even=
 on mobile, the top of the page is a flick or 2 away mostly.</li>=0A<li><st=
rong>Put relevant links in context</strong>: For example, add a subscribe f=
orm to the end of a post or add a CTA to a pricing section.</li>=0A<li><str=
ong>Use a back-to-top link</strong>: They=E2=80=99re relatively unobtrusive=
 (but only do this once you exhaust the other options).</li>=0A</ol></block=
quote>=0D=0A                                      =0D=0A                   =
